  "recommendations": {
    "operational_confidence_threshold": 0.25,
    "recommendation_evidence": "The empirical sensitivity analysis demonstrates remarkable stability of the Drone Health Score across the entire confidence spectrum. Moving from conf=0.25 to conf=0.40 yields an average absolute score shift of only 0.58 percentage points, with an exceptional Pearson correlation (r = 0.9994) and Spearman rank correlation (rho = 0.9979). 98.8% of images (494/500) remain in the identical health category. Even when escalating to conf=0.75, the mean score shift is only 1.69 percentage points, Pearson r remains 0.9958, and 95.6% of images maintain their exact category. Furthermore, detection confidence across all five classes is exceptionally high (dataset-wide median 0.9315, with Healthy at 0.9344 and Dead at 0.9321). Increasing the threshold to 0.50 or higher discards valid small and chlorotic palm detections without any measurable gain in diagnostic consistency. Therefore, the operational baseline threshold of 0.25 is retained with strong empirical support.",
    "retain_baseline": true
  },
  "limitations": [
    "RGB imagery captures visible phenotypic crown traits (color, crown size, mortality) but lacks near-infrared (NIR) and red-edge spectral bands required for physiological vegetation indices (NDVI/NDRE).",
    "Drone Health Score is an image-level metric; it cannot be merged at the block level because MOPAD Site 2 UAV tiles lack georeferenced GIS shapefiles, affine transforms, and plantation Block_ID mappings.",
    "Replanted or juvenile palm stands where fronds are classified as 'Small' register a 0% Drone Health Score under the current Healthy % definition, despite trees being alive and non-diseased."
  ]
